/**
  @page examples-geometry-uniformgrid Generating uniform grids
  @brief Control vertex counts, cell types, and physical dimensions.

  `Mesh::UniformGrid` creates a structured mesh without an input file. The
  grid parameters count vertices along each coordinate direction. Coordinates
  initially have unit spacing; physical dimensions are set by scaling the mesh.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-geom-ug-2d Create a two-dimensional grid

  Sixteen vertices in each direction give fifteen intervals. Splitting each
  rectangular grid cell into two triangles produces 450 triangles and
  256 vertices.

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <iostream>
  #include <Rodin/Geometry.h>

  using namespace Rodin;
  using namespace Rodin::Geometry;

  int main()
  {
    Mesh mesh = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});
    mesh.scale(1.0 / 15.0);

    Real area = 0;
    for (auto cell = mesh.getCell(); !cell.end(); ++cell)
      area += cell->getMeasure();

    std::cout << "Vertices: " << mesh.getVertexCount() << '\n';
    std::cout << "Triangles: " << mesh.getCellCount() << '\n';
    std::cout << "Area: " << area << '\n';
    return 0;
  }
  @endcode

  Before scaling, the grid occupies @f$[0,15]^2@f$. After scaling, it occupies
  @f$[0,1]^2@f$ and has area one. Scaling preserves the mesh connectivity and entity counts.

  The cell type determines how each grid rectangle is represented:

  | Type and vertex counts | Vertices | Cells |
  | :--- | ---: | ---: |
  | `Triangle`, `{16, 16}` | 256 | 450 triangles |
  | `Quadrilateral`, `{32, 32}` | 1024 | 961 quadrilaterals |

  For unequal vertex counts @f$n_x,n_y@f$, the initial domain is
  @f$[0,n_x-1]\times[0,n_y-1]@f$. A scalar scaling factor preserves that aspect
  ratio. The parameters must provide at least two vertices in each direction.

  @section examples-geom-ug-3d Extend to three dimensions

  Supply three vertex counts and choose a volume cell type.

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh tetrahedra = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Tetrahedron, {8, 8, 8});
  Mesh hexahedra = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Hexahedron, {8, 8, 8});
  Mesh wedges = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Wedge, {8, 8, 8});
  @endcode

  These grids initially occupy @f$[0,7]^3@f$. Calling `scale(1.0 / 7.0)` on
  each mesh maps it to the unit cube. The selected type changes the cells
  inside each grid brick; the three parameters remain vertex counts.

  @section examples-geom-ug-connectivity Prepare boundary operations

  Boundary algorithms require the relevant incidence relation. Exterior-edge
  detection in a triangular two-dimensional mesh uses edge-to-cell connectivity.

  @code{.cpp}
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2);
  @endcode

  For a three-dimensional volume mesh, face-to-cell connectivity is `(2, 3)`.
  See @ref examples-geometry-connectivity for the interpretation of these queries.

  @section examples-geom-ug-queries Check the generated geometry

  `getVertexCount()` and `getCellCount()` check topology. Summing cell
  `getMeasure()` values checks the area of a two-dimensional mesh, as shown
  above. `Mesh::getVolume()` sums the measures of three-dimensional entities. `getDimension()` and `getSpaceDimension()` distinguish
  topological from ambient dimension. These checks help detect an unintended
  domain size before a field or PDE is constructed.

  @section examples-geom-ug-full Source example

  @ref Geometry/UniformGrid.cpp "UniformGrid.cpp" generates a three-dimensional
  wedge mesh and saves it in MEDIT format. The @ref examples-pdes-poisson
  "Poisson tutorial" uses a scaled triangular grid in a complete solve.

  @section examples-geom-ug-see-also Further reading

  - @ref guides-meshes-creation — Mesh creation and loading.
  - @ref guides-polytopes — Reference elements and physical mappings.
  - @ref guides-connectivity — Incidence relations and boundary queries.

  @example Geometry/UniformGrid.cpp
  @m_examplenavigation{examples-geometry-uniformgrid,Geometry/}
  @m_footernavigation
*/
